	<!-- FONTS: use a system/logical family (SansSerif, Monospaced) - NOT a bundled .ttf.

	     Swing's text views position the caret using FontMetrics from an identity-transform
	     FontRenderContext, but glyphs are painted through the Graphics2D device transform (2x on
	     Retina).  A font loaded via Font.createFont() quantizes its advance widths against the
	     *device* pixel grid, so measured width and painted width disagree and the error accumulates
	     left-to-right - the caret drifts further from the text the longer the text gets.  Bundled
	     Inter at 15pt was ~20px (about 3 characters) off over an 82-char caption, and no point size
	     avoids it: measured drift ran 9-41px across sizes 11-24.  On macOS logical families go
	     through CoreText, which ignores the device transform, so they measure identically at every
	     display scale.  Enforced by StylesFontTest.

	     This fixes macOS only.  Linux and Windows have no CoreText - every font there uses the
	     FreeType scaler and drifts (measured on Linux: SansSerif 26-36px, Monospaced 27-59px), so
	     HiDPI users on those platforms can still see it.  No font choice helps; that would need a
	     custom TextUI measuring at the device scale.  At 100% scaling there is no drift anywhere.

	     Trade-off accepted: logical families resolve per-platform (Helvetica on macOS, Arial on
	     Windows - metrically compatible; Linux depends on fontconfig, usually DejaVu Sans, which is
	     wider).  Check fixed pixel widths on Linux: NAME_COL_WIDTH in PhotogenEditorPhase and the
	     caption box width passed to OptionTextArea. -->

